WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:05.880
To absolutely no one’s surprise or at least to&nbsp;
non-die-hard MCU fans, The Marvels is flat-out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:11.160
bad. Your enjoyment will obviously vary off of&nbsp;
purely how much fun you have with the movie and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:15.640
how much of the humor lands for you personally.&nbsp;
Because at the end of the day, that sort of thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:20.760
is extremely subjective. But for this review,&nbsp;
I’m going to primarily be analyzing the quality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:26.240
of the character work, plot, and overall writing&nbsp;
and why I think it’s a failure. Because to me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:30.920
those are the most important aspects that make&nbsp;
me fundamentally care about a film. Some jokes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:35.720
and a happy tone with a fast pace won’t make&nbsp;
me automatically love a movie and I can see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:40.000
why people can enjoy this significantly&nbsp;
more than something like Secret Invasion.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.400
So the biggest and most flawed aspect to me&nbsp;
was the character work. Out of the collection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:50.000
of characters within the movie between Carol&nbsp;
Danvers, Monica Rambeau, Kamala Khan, Nick Fury,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.480
and the villain, there was only one character that&nbsp;
actually felt well executed which was Kamala Khan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:00.320
Iman Vellani breathes so much energy and charisma&nbsp;
into Kamala that she’s always an extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:05.600
endearing and lovable character. Her bubbly and&nbsp;
fan-girly persona gives the character so much life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:11.080
to the point that the contrast between Monica and&nbsp;
Carol feels massive. Although that kind of dynamic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:16.440
between these characters is pretty likable because&nbsp;
of the sheer difference in energy and personality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:21.440
However, I think this also exposes how much&nbsp;
of a charisma sink both Carol and Monica are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:27.760
as characters or even actors. Carol, I would say&nbsp;
is a bit more likable here than in her own movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:32.920
and she’s showing some personality, but she still&nbsp;
comes across as being pretty bland. And the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:38.000
thing applies for Monica. They’re supposed to be&nbsp;
the more grounded and serious characters, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:43.280
the movie doesn’t offer enough depth for them to&nbsp;
really become interesting. For Kamala, you care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:47.720
so much about her because she’s still a kid and&nbsp;
there are stakes for her well-being because her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:52.920
family cares about her. Along with her vibrant and&nbsp;
unique perspective of absolutely loving becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:58.360
a superhero with her literal hero growing up. It&nbsp;
energizes her with a significant likability that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:03.040
Monica and Carol come nowhere close to. Because&nbsp;
the only thing those two characters have is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:08.400
minor past conflict in that Carol never came back&nbsp;
to help raise Monica. So since that development&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:13.600
is awkwardly dealt with and is basically breezed&nbsp;
over within a few minutes of it being brought up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:18.600
there’s really nothing the movie does to make&nbsp;
you care about these two other main characters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:23.320
That’s why I think Kamala absolutely carries the&nbsp;
film. Without going into spoilers, there’s this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:28.200
past action that Carol did that has come back to&nbsp;
haunt her, but the movie doesn’t really dive into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:33.000
that and is only dealt with at surface level. The&nbsp;
writers could have done a lot more internally and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:38.360
emotionally to make Carol Danvers a significantly&nbsp;
more relatable character. Like she may be this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:43.880
unstoppable god-like figure, but these major&nbsp;
choices she makes have consequences and she has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:49.240
to personally deal with the heavy guilt associated&nbsp;
with that. However, the movie essentially briefly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:55.360
brushes over this and doesn’t do much with that to&nbsp;
make Carol a more interesting character. So to me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:00.720
1 of the 3 leads was enjoyable while the other&nbsp;
2 came across as being bland and mostly dull.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:05.800
From here it gets significantly worse because the&nbsp;
handling of Nick Fury and the villain were truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:12.640
horrendous. Nick Fury is literally delegated as&nbsp;
a comedic relief character as 95% of his screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:17.840
time is just making quips and jokes. This is&nbsp;
obviously very jarring especially coming off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:23.320
of Secret Invasion in which Nick Fury is supposed&nbsp;
to have his groove back and I assume be at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:28.680
kind of serious, but nope, that’s not the case&nbsp;
at all. Every single moment he’s used to rattle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:34.160
off jokes and to be honest some of them landed&nbsp;
because I love Samuel L Jackson, but the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:38.320
time it was happening I couldn’t help but think&nbsp;
the writers were ruining the character. He’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:43.360
slowly being corrupted for short-term comedic&nbsp;
value as this brutally serious character turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:48.320
into a fluffy teddy bear. There’s really not much&nbsp;
else done with his character as he kind of just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:54.240
meanders around in a background subplot. Which&nbsp;
I’ll get into in a minute. Then finally we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:59.160
the villain. Now Marvel is extremely inconsistent&nbsp;
with their villain quality where they could either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:05.280
make complete masterpieces like Loki or Thanos, or&nbsp;
they could make atrocities. And the villain who I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:10.840
don’t even remember her name which is honestly&nbsp;
really sad. Apparently, her name is Dar-Benn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:16.080
is sadly in the atrocity tier. It didn’t even feel&nbsp;
like the writers tried to make her a compelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:21.760
villain. She’s just a bland, comically bad person&nbsp;
who has a motivation that is so devoid of logic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:26.200
that I don’t think it really constitutes as a&nbsp;
spoiler so I’ll go into it. For this segment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:30.880
I’m going to talk about the villain’s motivation&nbsp;
which is that Carol Danvers does an action that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:36.560
negatively impacts a planet, and now the sun is&nbsp;
dying. I don’t remember exactly why that is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:41.560
case since it was explained pretty quickly or if&nbsp;
it even was at all. But the first major thing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:46.160
wouldn’t make sense about this is that: if your&nbsp;
sun is dying, then your planet’s temperature would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:51.920
plummet. Thus destroying the population. Because&nbsp;
the movie visually shows this sun as being almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:58.760
extinguished and if that was the case, I’m&nbsp;
sure the planet would be uninhabitable. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:03.360
also without an atmosphere, I think in the&nbsp;
beginning of the movie. So to me at least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:08.640
the entire motivation surrounding the villain&nbsp;
is nonsensical because it doesn’t make sense.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:12.880
So that paired with a villain that has&nbsp;
barely any screen time, is monotonous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:17.400
and the creators gave her like 1 minute of&nbsp;
backstory makes her one of the weakest villains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:23.000
in the MCU. Every character besides Kamala feels&nbsp;
deeply flawed or doesn’t have enough happening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:27.960
internally to make them intriguing. Which to me&nbsp;
is by far the biggest failure in that by having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:33.920
an uninteresting main character and villain it&nbsp;
makes the story completely unengaging. Both the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:39.000
driving force of the film and the overall conflict&nbsp;
doesn’t captivate the audience. So the only thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:44.440
that does is the humor and fun tone. And even&nbsp;
though Kamala is a clear standout because of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:48.640
I think the creators realized that and&nbsp;
relentlessly used her high energy in almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:53.640
every moment of the film to the point that it&nbsp;
kind of burned me out. Which I still like her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:58.000
but she can’t always be dialed up to 11. There&nbsp;
needs to be some kind of balance for the character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:04.240
and at times they do find that balance while in&nbsp;
others she can feel overutilized. So ironically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:09.400
this movie feels more centered around Kamala&nbsp;
even though Carol Danvers is the main character.

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:13.800
Moving away from the characters, let’s address&nbsp;
the main selling point of the movie which is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:19.400
quantum entanglement. Now all the science fiction&nbsp;
exposition is rattled off so quickly to the point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:24.040
that I don’t expect they cared if the audience&nbsp;
really understood it. Which is fine to a degree,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:29.000
because with a vague scientific statement that&nbsp;
sounds half accurate, I can buy this as being real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:34.440
within the universe. However, what immediately&nbsp;
drives me crazy is if said mechanic is extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:39.800
inconsistent in how it works. So in essence, if&nbsp;
one of these three characters use their power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:44.280
they switch places with someone else. I assume&nbsp;
it’s random and there’s not a link between certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:49.960
characters. Now the inconsistency arrives in the&nbsp;
fact that in almost every single scene, one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:54.480
our characters uses their powers and doesn’t&nbsp;
trade places with another character. They’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:59.000
simply be using it to fight someone casually&nbsp;
as if there’s no entanglement happening, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:01.320
is an utter contradiction. This happens pretty&nbsp;
regularly in different fight scenes. At one point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:06.480
during a training montage, they establish that if&nbsp;
they keep using their power in a sustained sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:11.320
after teleporting then they won’t immediately do&nbsp;
it again. It was explained very fast and I wish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:16.600
I could rewatch it. But they constantly stop using&nbsp;
their power and then use it again. Or they’ll just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:22.480
be using their power and no teleportation happens.&nbsp;
So overall this leads to a completely inconsistent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:26.560
usage of the teleporting mechanic to the point&nbsp;
that it just feels like the creators are doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:31.320
it whenever they feel like it. This aspect was&nbsp;
really driving me crazy and since this mechanic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:36.160
was kind of a mystery for the characters, they&nbsp;
couldn’t actually explain it. So you just have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:41.360
roll with it and accept how it’s used in the movie&nbsp;
despite it being confusing and contradictory. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:46.440
leads into another major flaw in that it makes&nbsp;
some of the action scenes jarring. Specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:50.360
the ending scene in which all 3 characters&nbsp;
are attacking the villain simultaneously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:54.840
with their powers. It becomes really hard to&nbsp;
follow the action because the scene both has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.840 --> 00:08:00.000
really fast-paced editing with quick cuts, has&nbsp;
the teleporting mechanic randomly kicking in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:06.200
and 4 characters all fighting at once. The action&nbsp;
because of this quickly becomes overwhelming and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:10.600
thus is why I think it’s pretty jarring. There&nbsp;
is one action scene towards the beginning of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:15.040
movie in which the 3 main characters are in&nbsp;
different locations as they fight 3 separate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:20.720
battles. That was more digestible since each&nbsp;
set piece had a single hero fighting at a time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:25.960
but again, the teleporting mechanic quickly&nbsp;
became erratic in how it was used. So the big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:30.000
issue here is that this was one of the major&nbsp;
selling points of the movie and I think it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:34.520
handled pretty poorly. Even though I did&nbsp;
partially enjoy that first action scene.

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:39.040
Now going into the plot of the movie, there are&nbsp;
certain parts about it that I want to address.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:43.680
Firstly, this isn’t really a spoiler but there&nbsp;
is a portion of this movie that is dedicated to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:48.800
the skrulls. Given that Secret Invasion sets up&nbsp;
there being peace talks with the Kree. Now the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:53.480
first thing that is immediately contradicting&nbsp;
is that there are skrull outposts on different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.480 --> 00:08:58.640
planets. I thought the entire conundrum in Secret&nbsp;
Invasion was that they couldn’t find any habitable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:04.040
planet which is stupid in of itself, and they had&nbsp;
to go to earth. Which is inherently a contrivance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:09.880
to instill a false sense of conflict on earth.&nbsp;
However, here, through an utter contradiction, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:15.360
Skrulls are living on other planets albeit to a&nbsp;
small degree I guess. So this entire dynamic with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:20.280
the Skrulls is confusing because of this, along&nbsp;
with us not knowing these specific Skrulls so the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:25.800
politics between them and the Kree just led me to&nbsp;
not care. I’d assume a character like G’iah would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:30.320
be greatly involved with this kind of conflict&nbsp;
considering that she’s just a more OP version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:35.360
Captain Marvel, but I doubt the writers would even&nbsp;
consider that. So this entire chunk of the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:40.520
felt very underdeveloped and the Skrulls then&nbsp;
evaporate out of the movie. The only redeeming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.520 --> 00:09:44.960
aspect about this sequence is that it introduces&nbsp;
a character flaw for Carol in that her actions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:51.040
can have negative consequences and she isn’t this&nbsp;
almighty being who can only do good. Even though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:55.720
as I said before, I don’t think the writers&nbsp;
explore that angle of her character enough. Then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.720 --> 00:10:00.800
before going into the main plot, there are two set&nbsp;
pieces that will primarily be talked about a lot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:05.920
One of them is a planet in which the only language&nbsp;
people speak is singing. Although they’re singing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:13.080
in English, but they don’t understand English&nbsp;
if it’s not in song format. So yeahhh. So to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:17.160
put simply, this feels like the writers were high&nbsp;
and were trying to come up with a quirky gimmick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:22.120
for a planet and it didn’t land for me at all.&nbsp;
Then for the second, it’s tied directly to Nick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:26.720
Fury’s subplot which feels largely pointless and&nbsp;
I can’t really talk about it in detail because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:32.400
it spoils the ending of it. But talking about&nbsp;
it vaguely, it was so crazy and unhinged from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:37.720
writing perspective that I just ironically found&nbsp;
it funny. It’s going to be polarizing because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:42.520
how ridiculous the scene is, but I just lost&nbsp;
it. Especially at the music they used for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.520 --> 00:10:47.960
scene. So I hate to admit it, I kind of enjoyed&nbsp;
that scene the most, even though it was batshit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.960 --> 00:10:53.640
insane and I kind of respect the writers for doing&nbsp;
something that was that unhinged. It made me laugh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:58.840
but at the expense of breaking reality. But yeah,&nbsp;
the main plot for the movie is very stereotypical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:03.320
and plays it safe. The heroes essentially come&nbsp;
together through the first half of the movie and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:08.440
then they simply overcome the main conflict. At&nbsp;
no point is there any clever writing or story/plot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.160 --> 00:11:14.240
subversions and it is mostly a by-the-numbers&nbsp;
Marvel movie. Except it has a much faster pace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:19.440
which is both a blessing and kind of curse in&nbsp;
that we at least got out of the movie faster and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:24.200
they also couldn’t take a lot of time to build&nbsp;
characters. Like Carol desperately needed some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:28.640
more character development and I would have been&nbsp;
fine if they slowed down more for that. There are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:33.000
definitely more minor aspects about the story that&nbsp;
are poorly written but I think I went through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:38.200
biggest issues being the badly written characters,&nbsp;
the quantum entanglement mechanic being confusing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.200 --> 00:11:43.720
and inconsistent, and the plot being mediocre&nbsp;
with a plethora of dumb writing beats. So I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.720 --> 00:11:48.240
see why Marvel fans can have a fun time with&nbsp;
this movie, but anyone who is on the fence or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:53.800
isn’t loving the MCU anymore clearly won’t enjoy&nbsp;
this movie. Unlike Guardians of the Galaxy 3 which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.800 --> 00:11:58.560
was pretty much universally loved by everyone.&nbsp;
I’ll be trying to review Loki season 2 as soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:03.520
as possible and I actually enjoy that show. I&nbsp;
don’t purely hate the MCU and I wish it can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:15.120
better. Thank you for watching and let me know in&nbsp;
the comments what you thought about The Marvels.

